h-mad/SKILL.md:491:  **Then run the wire-pin gate — 5b is the last gate that can require it.** `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_wire_pin_gate.py docs/01-plan/features/<feature>.impl-plan.md --feature <feature>`. Read the `WIREPIN:` token, never `$?` (`UNSHAPED` exits 2 because it is a cannot-judge, not a verdict). On `WIREPIN: PASS`, 5b automatically registers each passing `wiring` task in `.h-mad/wires.jsonl`; without `--feature`, registration is skipped. `UNREADABLE` → halt `step5b:impl_plan_unreadable`: the gate could not read the file at all, so nothing was parsed — this token carries **no `tasks=`/`wiring=` counts** precisely so it cannot be mistaken for a verdict or routed by a count that was never measured; the stderr `ERROR:` names the path, which is almost always wrong rather than the plan. `UNSHAPED` → **read the `tasks=` count before choosing a remedy**: `tasks=N` with N>0 → halt `step5b:impl_plan_unshaped`, the plan declares no `Task shape` at all, so a wiring task in it is indistinguishable from new behaviour — return to 5a and **re-dispatch `implplan-author`** against the current template (§"Teammate authors"; this is a revision like any other, and regenerating it in your own context is the loop that cost 34 cycles); `tasks=0` → halt `step5b:impl_plan_no_tasks`, the parser saw no task header, so nothing is missing a field — you are almost certainly pointed at a legacy `.plan.md` or the design doc rather than the `.impl-plan.md`. `FAIL` has two causes and two different remedies, so **read the detail lines, not just the verdict**: an `unpinned:` line → halt `step5b:wire_pin_missing:<task>` and return to 5a to name the `WIRE`/`WIRE-PIN`; a `mislabeled:` line → halt `step5b:wire_pin_shape_mislabel:<task>` — the fields are already filled in, so nothing is missing to add and the missing-pin remedy would read as already satisfied. After 5b nothing downstream can tell a wired build from an unwired one, which is why the obligation is mechanical here and advisory nowhere.
h-mad/SKILL.md:496:- **5d** — RED dispatch (see `references/codex-implementer-prompt.md`). **Stage it with `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py --phase red` rather than by hand** — it cuts §"Task N" from the impl-plan, fills the template, and prints the exact command block with the model, the scoped test path and the probed interpreter already baked in; hand-assembly produced five distinct invocation mistakes across two sessions, every one a default. It refuses a 5d without the stated counts, which is the one judgement here that must stay yours. **Before the feature's first RED dispatch, run `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_prescribed_block_guards.py docs/01-plan/features/<feature>.impl-plan.md --repo-root <project-root>` and read its `PRESCRIBED-BLOCKS:` token**: it appends each python block the impl-plan prescribes for an existing test module to a scratch copy and runs that module's self-source guards against it. `FAIL` means the RED would come back over its stated count, one failure per distinct guard named, for a reason the plan wrote, so re-dispatch `implplan-author` with each `COLLISION:` line, unless the guard shows the block replaces code instead of adding it (a count guard over-reports there). `INCOMPLETE` checked less than it says; name what did not run. Guards the plan itself adds do not exist yet and are not checked. pin-agents-tail-banner passed 53 audit cycles carrying such a block. **Transport: default `hmad-dispatch exec codex` for a one-shot RED** — hard exit code, no scrape, monitor via `--log` (§"Exit-code dispatch for 5d/5e"); use the pane path (`send`) only for an iterative revision loop. The pane-path steps in the rest of this bullet (alive-check, context clear) apply only when you use `send`; `exec` is a fresh subprocess and needs neither. For the pane path: verify codex + agy are alive by **reading `hmad-dispatch env` and requiring `PREFLIGHT: PASS`** — do not chain `alive codex && alive agy`, which branches on `$?` and is the exact habit §"Audit-gate signal discipline" forbids (`env` deliberately returns 0 even on `PREFLIGHT: FAIL`, so a `&&` chain reads a failure as success). Refuse on anything but `PASS` → halt `step5d:no_<agent>_pane`. **Immediately after confirming each pane is alive, clear its context** (see §"Agent-pane context hygiene") so no prior-feature/prior-cycle conversation bleeds into this feature's TDD. For each module, dispatch Codex for tests; dispatch agy for coverage review. **In the dispatch, state the expected failing/passing counts for the task and label any regression guards** — tests asserting behaviour that already works, which must pass from the first run. Read each `STATUS:` with `h_mad_extract_verdict.py` (§"Reading a dispatch verdict"); no extractable verdict after a re-read and re-dispatch → halt `step5d:no_verdict:<module>`. Then verify the results **match the stated counts**; halt `step5d:red_not_all_failing` when an unlabelled test passes without implementation.
h-mad/SKILL.md:501:- **5e** — GREEN dispatch (`references/codex-implementer-prompt.md` + `references/agy-spec-reviewer-prompt.md`). **Stage the Codex half with `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py --phase green`** (the agy spec-review half is still assembled by hand). Pass `--mutation-spec <h-mad/tests/mutation-specs/<module>.json>` so GREEN appends one mutation row per guard it writes and runs `--check-anchors` on it (the only harness mode the Codex Phase-5 gate admits); without the flag GREEN lists the rows in its final message. GREEN proposes rows and the orchestrator still runs the harness and scores them. Note the sandbox rule it enforces for you: never dispatch a pytest-running phase with `--sandbox read-only` — it kills pytest's tempdir, so the run passes without measuring anything. The same applies to the anti-gaming verifier dispatch below, which the assembler does not yet stage. **Transport: default `hmad-dispatch exec` for a one-shot GREEN + its review** (§"Exit-code dispatch for 5d/5e"); use the pane path (`send`) for the iterative revision loop where cycles 2..N reuse the warm thread — there the alive-check + context-clear below apply. For the pane path: re-verify the Codex + agy panes alive and **clear each pane's context** (§"Agent-pane context hygiene") before the first GREEN dispatch of a feature. For each module, dispatch Codex for implementation; dispatch agy for spec-compliance review. Read both verdicts with `h_mad_extract_verdict.py` (§"Reading a dispatch verdict") — never by grepping the scrape for the halt value, which turns an agent's silence into a pass. If agy returns `VERDICT: DRIFT` → halt `step5e-review:spec_drift:<module>`. If no verdict can be extracted after a re-read and re-dispatch → halt `step5e:no_verdict:<module>`. On 3rd consecutive GREEN failure → halt `step5e:green_unreachable:<module>`. **After GREEN + spec-review, run the independent anti-gaming verification** (`references/codex-verifier-prompt.md`): module count → test-discrimination audit → quote the source line for each pinned property → full suite vs the reference, and cross-check every reported count a second way. Codex GREEN says the code was written; agy says it matches the spec; this pass answers "are the tests real and does the source hold the pinned properties" — the axis a green suite and a `STATUS: DONE` cannot self-certify (an agent can emit `DONE` over a fabricated count). A false property or a suite failure → halt `step5e:verify_failed:<module>`.
h-mad/SKILL.md:526:  **For a `wiring` task the whole-module revert is not sufficient — run the wire-scoped revert too.** Revert the **connection only** (the call site / import / registration / propagated argument), leaving the callee *and* the tests intact, and confirm the `WIRE-PIN` test fails. Module suite still green → halt `step5e:wire_unenforced:<module>`. The whole-module revert removes both sides, so its RED split returns identically whether or not the wire exists; it is structurally incapable of establishing the one decision a wiring task ships. **Assert the revert landed before trusting the run** — use `git stash push -u -- <paths>` (the sequence above; `-u` is what reaches an untracked file, and `git add -N` breaks it rather than enabling it) and read the result back with an **existence** check, since `git diff --quiet` is trivially clean for an untracked path and cannot see this failure. A revert that never happened reports as a pass. Then mutate the other direction: force the connection to fire unconditionally and confirm the fall-through/negative test fails. See `invariants.base.md` §"Connection enforcement". **Record authors before review:** when the orchestrator accepts a module's GREEN dispatch (reads `STATUS: DONE` or `STATUS: DONE_WITH_CONCERNS` and stops re-dispatching GREEN), run `h_mad_state_write.py docs/.bkit-memory.json --feature <feature> --session-id <owner-session-id> --append implementation_authors='{"module":"<module>","agent":"<agent>"}'` before the module's 5e spec-review leg runs. Use the accepted dispatch's resolved `TDD-IMPLEMENTER:` agent, passed as `--agent` when staging (or `claude` for the in-session fallback), as described in item 7. Every accepted fix dispatch after a 5e review or verification finding appends the same author entry before the next leg that reviews the fixed code. Encode actual values as JSON and shell-quote the complete argument. An unaccepted dispatch appends nothing; a refused append must be corrected and retried before review. An accepted GREEN's entry remains if a later verification step halts.
h-mad/SKILL.md:527:- **5f** — **Derive the 5c sha, never `git merge-base`.** `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_baseline_sha.py --branch <feature-branch> [--trunk main]`, reading the `BASELINE:` token, never `$?`. 5c is `git checkout -b …; commit impl-plan + audit files`, so the 5c commit is the branch's FIRST commit and `git merge-base main <branch>` returns the commit it forked FROM — off by exactly the impl-plan commit, and off plausibly, since both are real shas on the right branch. Measured (J41): merge-base `b5c8f41` against real 5c `730cc16`, 313 lines apart — the impl-plan and its three audits, which the wrong base feeds into the review as newly-added content when they are a separate INPUT to it. `UNVERIFIED` is a verdict, not an error: the branch's first commit does not touch an impl-plan, so something was committed before 5c and the rule does not hold — find the commit by hand, and note the token reports it as `candidate=` rather than `sha=` precisely so an unvouched value cannot be scraped as the answer. Only `OK` carries `sha=`. Then, from the repository root, re-verify the registry with `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_wire_registry.py verify --base <5c sha> --rootdir <repository-root> --testpath <project-test-root>` (for this repository, `h-mad/tests` is an example), then run `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_wire_registry.py challenge --base <5c sha>`. `--rootdir` is the repository root (pytest's cwd), while `--testpath <project-test-root>` must name a collectable test root whose node ids are repo-relative; use both to avoid collecting this repository root's pre-existing sibling-project import mismatches. The challenge is **warning-only and verdict-neutral**: an unproven heuristic never blocks an operator. The verifier's halt reasons are `step5f:wire_regression:<feature>::<id>`, `step5f:wire_pin_missing:<feature>::<id>`, `step5f:wire_pin_ambiguous:<feature>::<id>`, `step5f:registry_untracked`, `step5f:undeclared_removal:<feature>::<id>`, and `step5f:unverified_rename:<feature>::<id>`; each is emitted with `[H-MAD]`. **A `pin` may be a single test or a LIST of them**, because one wiring task can wire N call sites and one pin per record could only ever prove site 1 — the rest were left to ACs and the wire-scoped revert, which the registry cannot see. A record resolves only when EVERY pin resolves to exactly one node id, and is verified only when EVERY pin passes: a partial pass would report a wire as proven while a site it names went unchecked. Ambiguity outranks absence, since a pin naming more than one test must be qualified before the record can be judged at all. **On a multi-pin record the halt reason gains a `#<pin>` suffix** — `step5f:wire_pin_missing:<feature>::<id>#<pin>` — and one line is emitted per offending pin; a single-pin record's reason is unchanged. Without the suffix two broken sites on one record emit the same line twice and name neither, which is J43's defect one level down. `register --pin` is repeatable, and stores a bare string when given exactly once, so every record written before the list form reads back unchanged. **The registry's identity is `(owning_feature, id)`, never `id` alone** — `id` is the impl-plan task number and every impl-plan numbers its tasks from 1, so a bare id names no single wire once two features have run. Keyed on the bare id, registration silently upserted one feature's wire over another's and the comparison, keyed the same way, saw the successor and reported nothing — performing exactly the removal the `status`/`removal_provenance`/`removed_by_feature` tombstone fields exist to force an author to declare. Measured before the fix: 1 of the 7 records ever registered in this repository had already been lost that way, and it was unrestorable, because re-registering it evicted the successor (J43). Then run the full test suite: `pytest <project>/tests/ -v --tb=short`. All must pass (100%). Any failure → halt.
h-mad/SKILL.md:532:  **Run the mutations through `h_mad_mutation_harness.py` rather than by hand.** `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_mutation_harness.py <spec.json>`, where the spec names the suite command and each mutation as an exact `find`/`replace`. Read the `MUTATION:` token, never `$?`. The harness exists because the hard part of this pass is not choosing mutations, it is proving each one **landed**: an anchor that matches zero times mutates nothing, the suite stays green, and the run reports the guard as enforced — the failure `invariants.base.md` §"Mutation verification" names, and one every hand-rolled harness has to re-derive. It refuses any anchor not found exactly once — printing `hint:` lines with the near-miss line numbers, so a drifted anchor no longer costs a manual re-grep — restores the tree on every path including an interrupt, and re-runs the suite afterwards to prove the restore landed. The mutation run performs the sibling sweep itself before the baseline command and refuses on sibling drift with `MUTATION: PRECHECK_FAILED specs=N drifted=K unreadable=J` (exit 2), so a stale sibling spec cannot be hidden by running only the spec you happened to touch. The standalone diagnostic remains available as `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_mutation_harness.py --check-anchors <spec.json>…` — or with **no spec at all**, which sweeps every committed spec in the repository (`git ls-files '*.json'` through the same classifier as the pre-push hook), so `h-mad/tests/specs/` and `handoff/tests/mutation-specs/` are reached without anyone remembering they exist; a habitual `tests/mutation-specs/*.json` once said ANCHORS_OK over a drifted `tests/specs/` spec — reading the `ANCHORS:` token, never `$?`; it applies nothing and runs no tests, so it costs file reads rather than a suite per spec, and it reads the same one-match rule the run does (`anchor_status`) rather than a second copy of it. `ANCHORS_DRIFTED` and `ANCHORS_UNREADABLE` are **separate verdicts because the remedy differs** — re-anchor a spec, versus restore a file that is gone — and `ANCHORS_UNREADABLE` outranks, since a spec cannot be re-anchored into a file that no longer exists (J37); `ANCHORS_UNREADABLE` also covers a swept file that is not valid JSON at all, which judged nothing for the same reason; both exit 2 and both carry `drifted=`/`unreadable=` counts, so the word chooses the FIRST action without hiding the other finding. The `MUTATION:` summary gains `unreadable=` for the same split, but keeps ONE `REFUSED` word: it has five causes and a single remedy, so five verdicts would be worse than the disease. `ANCHORS_DRIFTED` → re-anchor before trusting any verdict from that spec: a drifted anchor mutates nothing, so its run REFUSES, and a refusal measures NOTHING. A relative spec `root` is spec-relative, never cwd-relative: it resolves against the spec file's directory rather than the caller's cwd, which makes the spec portable across checkouts and correct inside a git worktree. Measured 2026-08-26: 7 of 177 anchors across 14 committed specs had drifted, and every one of those guards was unverified while its spec still reported a verdict-shaped line — two of the seven were broken by a refactor made minutes earlier in the same session.
h-mad/SKILL.md:534:  **To claim a prompt rule WORKS, run it against a control** — `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_ab_dispatch.py --template <prompt-with-{{VAR}}> --var VAR --a "<rule>" --b "" --observe '<regex with ONE capture group>' --out <dir> --run <argv token>…` (`{prompt}`, `{log}` and `{arm}` are substituted per arm — **put `{arm}` in every OTHER per-arm path you pass**, or both arms share it and arm B silently overwrites arm A, after which the comparison reads B against B and reports `SAME`, J40/F5). **It does not control the environment**: the tool holds the PROMPT constant apart from the declared variable and knows nothing about the rest — a changed file on disk, a different model default, an env var — so `UNCONTROLLED` only ever compares the two prompts, and holding everything else equal is yours to do. An unstated limit reads as a guarantee, which is why this is stated (J40/F3), reading the `AB:` token, never `$?`. One dispatch cannot distinguish a rule that steered the agent from an agent that would have done the right thing anyway, so "the rule is present" gets recorded as "the rule is causally effective". Three verdicts carry it. `UNCONTROLLED` → the arms differ in more than the declared variable (or in nothing), which is the mistake a hand-run A/B actually makes, and it is silent — the run completes, the numbers differ, and the difference is attributed to the wrong cause; nothing is dispatched. `INCONCLUSIVE` → an arm produced no log or the observable never matched, and **two silent arms compare equal**, so this must never read as `SAME`. `SAME` is itself a finding: the rule is present and not causally effective. **The exit code is reported and never scored** — a dispatch killed by its parent shell, a skipped test and a clean run all exit 0, and this repo has been fooled by each. **`--observe` takes the LAST match, not the first**, on the same rule as `h_mad_extract_verdict.py`: an agent's log carries the PROMPT before it carries the answer, so a first-match read routinely returns the observable's own name quoted back out of the instruction — and since both arms echo the same prompt, both observe the same value and the run reports `SAME`, a *finding* rather than an error (J39, measured: a log reading `RESULT: 0` as the echo and `RESULT: 42` as the answer observed `0`). The regex needs exactly one capture group and is refused at the boundary otherwise. **`--run` accepts a dash-leading token in either form** — `--run --model` and `--run=--model` both work; the space-separated form documented here used to fail with `expected one argument` for every real dispatch argv, since they all start with flags (J38).
h-mad/SKILL.md:536:  **Parking a mutation spec (D2), and the detector for one never moved back (#142).** Mutation rows are written at GREEN against source that exists, never at RED; a spec drafted before its source lands is *unanchored*, and an unanchored spec under `tests/mutation-specs/` reds the committed-spec sweep and blocks the pre-push hook. Park it instead as `docs/03-analysis/<feature>.pending-mutation-specs/<name>.json.pending` and restore it to `tests/mutation-specs/<name>.json` at that task's GREEN. **The parked copy stays afterwards as provenance — do not delete it.** The name match is the whole convention, so name the parked file exactly as the live one will be named. What nothing used to detect is the other case: `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` now blocks with `pending_mutation_spec_not_restored` when a `<name>.json.pending` has no live `<name>.json` in any `tests/mutation-specs/` directory up to two levels below the repo root (`--mutation-specs-dir` names one explicitly), and warns `pending_specs_unverified` when it finds parked specs but no such directory at all — "I could not check" is not "never moved back". A parked spec's guards were never measured, and the sibling sweep cannot see it precisely because parking moved it out of the swept directory.
h-mad/SKILL.md:538:  **After the LAST edit of a rename or removal, sweep the old identifier across every surface** — `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_identifier_sweep.py <old-name>… --root <repo> [--allow <path>]…`, reading the `SWEEP:` token, never `$?`. It reports each remaining hit with its surface (code · comment · doc · test · mutation-anchor), so the surfaces that get missed unevenly are visible rather than remembered. `LEFTOVERS` is **not** a verdict that the hits are wrong: deciding explanation-vs-leftover is yours, and a deliberate one is passed with `--allow` rather than edited away — which is why the allowlist is an input and never inferred. Hits on the *stem* of a renamed file (`advisor-gate` after `advisor-gate.sh` is gone) are reported as `related:` and do not move the verdict, because prose naming the concept is both real signal and the sweep's main source of noise. The timing is the property: `a311385` renamed `hooks/h-mad-advisor-gate.sh` and shipped three stale references to a file the same commit deletes, after the sweep was started by hand mid-work and abandoned when more edits followed. Run at the end it costs a second; run during, it is unreliable in exactly the way that produced the defect. **Give every mutation a `test`** (plus a spec-level `target_command`) unless there is a reason not to: `ALL_CAUGHT` only says something went red, and a mutant killed by a crash, a timeout, or an unrelated assertion looks exactly the same as one killed by the guard. With a `test`, only that test counts as a kill and a wrong-catcher is reported as a SURVIVOR naming what actually bit. `SURVIVED` → halt `step5e:mutation_survived:<module>`: the guard does not bite, so write the discriminating test rather than weakening the mutation. `REFUSED` / `BASELINE_NOT_GREEN` / `UNREADABLE` → halt `step5e:mutation_unverified:<module>`: **nothing was measured**, which is not a pass — treat it exactly as you would a cannot-judge from any other gate.
