h-mad/SKILL.md:2473:- **Run the project suite with the gate in Phases 3, 4 and 5b, and read `SUITE:` as its own line (#91).** `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_audit_gate.py <report> --gated <doc> --project-tests <project-test-root> [--suite-cmd "<cmd>"]`. Measured on HemaSuite `#18 gateway-consolidation`: a dual-surface design audit ran **95 cycles** without meeting its exit gate while a repo test enforcing the **audit loop's own invariant** stayed red for the entire life of the feature — `tests/test_audit_phase_inline_summary_sync.py::test_real_features_synced`, whose guard is that every earlier cycle recording a FAIL must be cited in the Version History. It went green for the first time after 91+ cycles. The gate could not have caught it: before this it held **zero** occurrences of `subprocess|pytest|check_call|os.system`, and `pytest` appeared in this file only at 5e and 5f. **The root must be SCOPED** — an unscoped pytest from a repository root collects sibling-project import mismatches (step 5f says so, and HemaSuite carries two suites under a monorepo root). The verdict is scored on pytest's own **summary line, never on `$?`**: a skipped selection and a killed run both exit 0, and `SUITE: UNREADABLE reason=no_summary` is a cannot-judge that is neither a pass nor a failure.
h-mad/SKILL.md:2475:- **One measured run per working tree, and a report when the tree moved under it.** The concurrent-suite hazard above is no longer prose: every pytest session under this repository's rootdir takes the same per-toplevel tree lock as the mutation harness. The repository's root conftest registers the lock, so bare `pytest`, `pytest handoff/tests`, one `handoff/scripts` file and `pytest .` all take it, and `h-mad/tests/conftest.py` registers it for install-path runs. As a result, a second session over one tree prints `SUITE: BUSY holder=<pid> age=<s> what=<pytest-session|spec path> lock=<file>` and exits 75 before collecting anything. This gate reads that as `SUITE: UNREADABLE reason=suite_busy`, naming the holder on the next line, and the wire registry reads it as UNREADABLE. Neither reads it as a failure. **The lock also blocks governed writes.** While a suite holds the tree, the TDD gate's judge is refused too, so every governed Edit or Write is denied as `no-summary` with the `SUITE: BUSY` line in its reason until the suite ends. Start a background suite only when no governed edit is planned before it finishes. A tree whose lock cannot be written (a read-only checkout) prints `SUITE: LOCK_UNAVAILABLE` and runs unlocked rather than erroring. A harness started under a suite prints `MUTATION: BUSY … spec=pytest-session`, and a suite started under a harness is refused the same way. The harness's own inner pytest runs are exempt through the holder token it exports (`H_MAD_TREE_LOCK_HELD`); separate worktrees are separate toplevels and never contend on the lock. Tests that share fixed `/tmp` paths can still collide across worktrees. At session end the suite also compares a digest of the non-ignored tree with the one taken at start and prints `SUITE: TREE_MOVED paths=… n=K` when they differ, so a pass count measured over bytes that changed says so. The gate and the cycle driver carry it onto their `SUITE:` line as `tree_moved=<paths>`. It reports and never blocks an edit, it is quiet on ignored writes, and it cannot see an edit reverted before the run ends. The harness's own `TREE_MOVED` compares the same digest, not only HEAD. To tell a failure the change caused from one the base already has, use `h_mad_failure_diff.py run --base <ref> [--repeat N] -- <selection>` rather than a hand-built clean worktree: it runs the same selection in a throwaway detached worktree at the base, diffs the failure sets by node id, and reports a test that fails in only some runs as FLAKY (script inventory below).
h-mad/SKILL.md:2477:- **Drive the gate through `h_mad_audit_cycle.py` and pass the flags THERE — the driver feeds every leg.** `--project-tests <root> [--suite-cmd "<cmd>"] --gated <doc> [--legs <name> …]` on the *cycle driver*, not on each gate call. The suite runs **once per cycle** and its verdict is forwarded to every per-leg gate invocation as `--suite-result`; running it inside the gate would be minutes per leg and is the design `audit_suite_gate.json` rejects outright. The leg set is forwarded identically to every leg, so the legs of one cycle cannot disagree about what that cycle ran. Before this, `gate()` built its command with the collected report and `--ack-file` and nothing else, so **every cycle driven through the script recorded `suite: null` with no leg set** and its exit gate was refused `suite_unmeasured` — both gates shipped, correct, and unreachable from the path that actually runs. `--suite-result` and `--project-tests` are mutually exclusive on the gate: one field, one source. **`GATE_RE` now tolerates trailing fields** on line 1, because `--gated` appends ` gated=N` there; the gate's own comment had recorded that collision as latent "because the cycle driver never passes `--gated`", and wiring this made it live — the first gated run died with `ERROR: missing GATE token` against a gate that had answered `GATE: PASS must=0 should=0 gated=1`.
