h-mad/SKILL.md:3185:- `h_mad_check_memory_index.py` — pre-check a memory index against the caps Claude Code enforces at LOAD time: CLI printing `OK|WARN|OVER|UNREADABLE <path> bytes=N/25000 lines=N/200 worst=<dim> frac=N% dropped=N target=N`, exit 0 clean / 1 WARN-or-OVER / 2 unreadable. **It exists because the built-in warning is one write too late and the loss is silent**: past the cap the write SUCCEEDS and, in the binary's own words, *"everything past the limit is silently dropped each time the index is loaded — entries at the end are already invisible to readers"*. Nothing in the tree looks wrong and `wc -c` cannot see it. Caps are decoded from the binary (25000 bytes / 200 lines, warn at 0.8, compact to 0.7 = 17500/140) and **re-grepped by the test on every run**, which SKIPS rather than passes when the binary is unreadable. **Both dimensions are scored and the WORSE one binds** — an index at 40% of its byte cap can still be over on lines, so a byte-only check reports healthy on a broken index; that is the row its mutation spec calls load-bearing. `--show-dropped` prints the invisible text rather than a count, because `dropped=254` is a number and the two entries it is deleting are the finding. Default target is every `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/MEMORY.md` de-duplicated **by inode** — those directories are routinely symlinks onto ONE store (measured: 40+ paths, one inode), and without the de-dup a single problem prints dozens of times. Wired as a pre-check in `handoff/references/auto-memories.md`. Stdlib-only.
h-mad/SKILL.md:3186:- `h_mad_precheck_doc.py` — phase-document pre-dispatch precheck (§"Precheck before you dispatch"): `scan()` + CLI printing `PRECHECK: PASS|FAIL issues=N|UNREADABLE`, exit 0 on a verdict / 2 on operational error. Refutes before the prompt is assembled what a cycle would otherwise spend two dispatches discovering. **Hard** findings are only the provably-wrong ones — `PLACEHOLDER` (an unfilled `key=…` or bare `<slot>`, impl-plan only, plus `TBD`/`TODO`/`FIXME` anywhere), `LINEPIN` past end-of-file (and, in `design`/`plan`/`spec`, ANY line pin — those author contracts forbid one, `NO_LINE_PINS` now enforces it, and `docs/archive/**` is exempt because an archived document cannot be repaired), `PINDRIFT` (a pin into a file that changed since the document's own newest provenance commit — the c33 defect exactly, six `SKILL.md` pins stale by 93 lines), and `UNKNOWNSHA` (a provenance sha naming no commit here). **Advisory, verdict-neutral**: `PATH` and `SYMBOL` (a planning document names files and symbols the feature will CREATE), `STALESHA` (behind-HEAD is the normal condition of every written measurement), ordinary `LINEPIN` **in `impl-plan` and in archived documents only**, and `COUNT`. Every one of those started as a hard finding and was demoted by measurement, not by taste: they fired 104, 49 and 48 times on the design and plan that had just passed 83 and 74 audit cycles, and the hits were correct usage. `--allow` is an input, never inferred — but **the `## Version History` demotion IS inferred, from the document's own content**, and it is the one exception: every hard finding inside that section is demoted to `allowed` (still printed, never dropped), because the section holds prose ABOUT changes and the detectors read it as the changes themselves. Measured: one impl-plan failed entirely on its own changelog saying "**TBD placeholders removed.**". The section is bounded by `h_mad_version_history`'s own notion — sole full-line `## Version History` heading, ending at the next header or `---`, fence-aware — and **two headings demote nothing**, because first-match on a document carrying a template history above its live one would silence the body between them. So a `PASS` may be a demoted `FAIL`; read the `ALLOWED:` lines. The residual it cannot close: a document that NARRATES a stale pin quotes the stale number, and no detector distinguishes that from the defect. Stdlib-only.
h-mad/SKILL.md:3187:- `h_mad_delta_review.py` — delta self-review over a revision's ADDED lines (#11/H4): `is_command_shaped()` / `is_executable()` / `added_lines()` / `claims()` + CLI printing one `DELTA: CLEAN|CLAIMS|UNREADABLE claims=N executed=E unverified=U` line, a `executed:`/`unverified:` line per claim with the source line, then the `[H-MAD]` marker. Exit 0 on a verdict, 2 on a cannot-judge. Executes only a bare read-only verb with no shell metacharacters, matched on the VERB and never as a substring; everything else is `unverified` and counted. Stdlib-only.
h-mad/SKILL.md:3188:- `h_mad_expect_screens.py` — run every published `# expect <N>` screen AT a freeze sha before certifying "no census moved" (#49w, `measurement-discipline.md` §FREEZE). CLI: `<doc.md>... --at <sha> --project-root ROOT [--timeout S]`. Documents are read from the working tree; every statement in a `bash`/`sh` fence ending `# expect <N>` (boundaries follow bash: a trailing `\`, a trailing `|`/`&&` across comments and blank lines, multi-line quotes, here-documents) is evaluated once per block in a throwaway detached worktree at the sha (in ROOT's subdirectory when ROOT is one), with stdin from /dev/null and `GIT_DIR`-style redirects dropped. The worktree is removed on every path including SIGTERM/SIGHUP, and only its own entry is deleted, never by a repo-wide prune; nothing runs in ROOT's working tree. One `screen: <doc>:<line> expect=<N> got=<value|UNREADABLE:reason> <PASS|FAIL>` line per screen, then `EXPECT: PASS screens=S` (exit 0), `EXPECT: FAIL screens=S failed=F unreadable=U` (exit 1), `EXPECT: CANNOT_JUDGE reason=…` (exit 2: usage, not a commit, unreadable doc, worktree failure) or `EXPECT: NONE` (exit 3: nothing tagged, nothing certified), then a `coverage:` line. A died or looped screen, a pipeline member exiting ≥2, any earlier statement that exited non-zero (blocks run under `set -o pipefail`; no status is judged benign from command text, so a deliberate no-match is filtered with a tool that exits 0 on no match — `awk '/PAT/'`, `sed -n '/PAT/p'`, or `awk '/PAT/{n++} END{print n+0}'` for a count — and needs no `||` (`|| true` masks every status, an upstream error included); reported as `failed_command=<doc>:<line>:<status>`), an `&&`/`||` list or `&` job at or before the screen, conditions and guards included (bash runs no ERR trap for those, so they are refused as `unsupported_construct=…`; every `||` is refused, with no declared form; exempt only: the `&` of a redirection, and a one-line `NAME=$(…)` whose body is a single `&&` chain; `&&