h-mad/SKILL.md:3202:- `h_mad_prescribed_block_guards.py` — pre-RED check of an impl-plan's prescribed python blocks against their target test module's own guards (§5d): CLI `<impl-plan.md> [--repo-root DIR]` printing `PRESCRIBED-BLOCKS: PASS|FAIL collisions=K|INCOMPLETE` with `unrunnable=U baseline_red=B checked=N skipped=M guards=G`, then `COLLISION:`/`BASELINE-RED:`/`UNRUNNABLE:`/`WRAPPED:` lines; exit 0 on a verdict, 2 on `UNREADABLE`. **Attribution**, in order: a block whose first-line comment LEADS with a test path belongs to it; a template `**Code structure**` block under a `**Production file**` belongs to that file, checked only when it is itself a test module; otherwise the prose since the previous fence or heading must name exactly one test path, and every path named must resolve (repo-relative, else by unique suffix, so a bare basename or a spec's subproject-relative path works; prose saying `create`/`new` gets no suffix match, so a new module is never resolved onto another sub-project's file). Mutation `find`/`replace` text is skipped; backtick and tilde fences, list-indented ones included, are scanned; an unclosed python fence is `UNRUNNABLE`. **Guards are selected by behaviour, not name** (only one of the repo's three is `*_guard`): a test that reads its own file — `read_text`/`read_bytes`/`open` of `__file__`, a path built from it, a name bound to either, or `<dir> / "<own name>"` — directly or through a module- or class-level helper or constant that does; never one that only locates siblings from it. Each runs first on the live module (red there is `BASELINE-RED`, not blamed on the block), then in a child process that imports the live module with its own-path reads through `open`/`io.open`/`Path.read_text`/`read_bytes` redirected to a temp copy carrying the block, so constants read at import, helpers and decorated guards all see it and `__file__` sibling lookups still work (`tokenize.open`, `linecache` and subprocesses bypass it). The block goes in verbatim when the module still parses with it (an `ast.parse` guard sees code), else as a string literal, reported `WRAPPED:`. The child's result travels in a file, never scraped from stdout, and it writes no bytecode. Not checked: guards the plan itself adds, which do not exist before RED. Measured on the 26 tracked impl-plans: 22 blocks attributed, 5 of them to a module that has guards, and one collision, real but not catchable pre-RED for that reason (`preflight-signal-discipline` prescribed a comment its own new guard bans; the implementer broke the literal by hand). The catchable case, pin-agents-tail-banner's helper (rewritten in place), is reproduced by a test against the live module. Stdlib-only; reuses `h_mad_doc_block_exec._fence_events`.
h-mad/SKILL.md:3203:- `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` — Phase-5d/5e Codex dispatch assembler: `assemble()` / `task_body()` + CLI printing `ASSEMBLE-TDD: PASS <promptfile> <bytes>B phase=<red|green|gate> task=<id> shape=<s>` followed by the verbatim command block (backgrounded `exec codex` with `--model`/`--effort`/`--out`/`--log`/`--timeout` → `progress` → `h_mad_extract_verdict.py --key STATUS` → an INDEPENDENT scoped pytest), exit 0 on PASS and on `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified` after `AGY-GATE: UNWIRED`, 2 on every other `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT <reason>`. It stages the prompt and prints the commands; **it does not dispatch** — the dispatch/poll/wait loop is §"Exit-code dispatch for 5d/5e", and a driver that dispatches either blocks blind for the timeout or re-implements `progress`. Cuts §"Task N" out of the impl-plan using the wire-pin gate's own `_TASK_RE`/`_parse_tasks` rather than a second parser (#39), and stamps the phase into the task section because the template carries BOTH the RED and the GREEN instructions and nothing in it says which applies. Halt reasons: `task_not_found`, `impl_plan_unreadable`, `template_unreadable`, `residual_slots`, `no_wire_pin`, `counts_required`, `interpreter_has_no_pytest`, `test_path_misrooted`, `sandbox_read_only`, `prompt_unwritable`, `env_known_failure_malformed`, `env_known_failures_unreadable`, `agy_tdd_hook_unverified` (`--agent agy` only: the agy wiring check did not print `AGY-GATE: WIRED`; exit 0 on `UNWIRED`, 2 on `UNREADABLE`, no token or a timeout). **Sandbox-only failures are declared, never hand-appended** (skill-monitoring J51): Codex's `workspace-write` sandbox denies loopback socket binding, so socket tests fail inside it and pass outside, and a correct RED reads over-count and returns `STATUS: BLOCKED`. `--env-known-failure <path>::<test>` (repeatable) or `--env-known-failures-file` (one per line, `#` comments) puts those node ids in the prompt. The prompt says the stated counts were measured outside the sandbox, that these ids must not be modified, and that any OTHER unexplained failure is still a stop. A whole file is refused (`env_known_failure_malformed`) because it would hide a real failure among the socket ones. The sandbox's network is NOT opened instead: `-c sandbox_workspace_write.network_access=true` does permit the bind, but it opens far more than loopback. The defaults exist because hand-assembly produced five distinct mistakes, each an invocation default rather than a judgement: a model pinned in the assembler at all — it outranks the codex CLI's own `$CODEX_HOME/config.toml`, so the model the TUI shows and the model 5d/5e runs drift apart; `--model`/`--effort` are OVERRIDES, emitted only when given (effort reaches codex as `-c model_reasoning_effort=<e>`), and the escape hatch they provide is real because a configured `gpt-5.6-luna` cannot execute tools and fails as a well-formed `STATUS: BLOCKED`; a bare `python3` (3.14 here, no pytest) — so the chosen interpreter is PROBED for pytest and a failure names a working one; the prompt passed inline when `exec` takes a FILE PATH; `--sandbox read-only` on a run that executes pytest, which kills pytest's tempdir so the pass measures nothing — refused; and an unscoped pytest that collects the sibling project, so `--test-path` is required and the prompt tells the agent not to widen it. `--test-path` is also **rooted**, which the interpreter always was and the path was not: it is interpolated verbatim into the prompt (run under `--cd <project-root>`) and into the operator's re-run line, so one string had to be correct relative to two roots. Given subproject-relative, pytest exits 4 on the missing file — or **5** when a same-named directory exists at the root, which runs, collects nothing and reports no error. Absence alone is NOT refused, because a 5d RED names the file the agent is about to write; a path that exists SOMEWHERE ELSE under the root is `test_path_misrooted`, and the refusal names the path that would have worked. The operator's re-run line now runs in a subshell `cd`'d to the project root. **What it refuses to guess is the judgement:** a 5d without `--expect-fail`/`--expect-pass` is `counts_required`, never defaulted, because "guard changed" and "test weakened" are otherwise indistinguishable. Supplying HALF a split — one of the pair without the other — is also `counts_required`, for **every shape and every phase**, including the two carve-outs that allow neither count: absent-BOTH is an exemption, absent-ONE is an incoherent invocation whose given half would reach no part of the prompt. A `wiring` task needs no counts — the RED split is identical either way — but its `WIRE`/`WIRE-PIN` are carried automatically and a missing pin is `no_wire_pin`. **Needing no counts is not the same as discarding the ones you gave.** Counts supplied for a `wiring` task are stated with an informational rider (the WIRE-PIN, not the split, is what gates it), and the regression-guard line is emitted for EVERY shape — guards are orthogonal to shape, and the label is what makes an immediate pass legitimate rather than something to manufacture away. Until 2026-09-21 the WIRE lines REPLACED the counts-and-guards block instead of preceding it, so `--expect-fail`/`--expect-pass`/`--guard` were parsed, validated and silently dropped on every `wiring` task while the run still ended `ASSEMBLE-TDD: PASS`. Filed from HemaSuite after four of one feature's wiring dispatches shipped with no stated split at all — rescued only by the orchestrator's habit of hand-writing a preamble, which was not correcting the spliced values but was their sole source. That habit is no longer load-bearing. Stdlib-only. Gate staging also halts with `test_path_required` when RED/GREEN has no `--test-path`, `phase_shape_mismatch` when `--phase gate` selects a task whose shape is not `gate`, or `counts_not_applicable` when a gate receives either expected count.
