h-mad/SKILL.md:3206:- `h_mad_pane_janitor.py` — post-probe pane cleanup: `snapshot` / `plan` / `clean` + CLI printing `JANITOR: PLANNED|CLEANED|NOTHING|SNAPSHOT candidates=N settled=J closed=K skipped=S` or `JANITOR: REFUSED reason=<r>`, exit 0 on a verdict / 2 on a refusal. **Dry run unless `--apply`.** Refusal reasons: `cannot_identify_self`, `baseline_unreadable`, `baseline_malformed`, `too_many_candidates`, `orca_unavailable`, `orca_unparseable`, `orca_error`. The hard part is not closing panes, it is deciding which panes are the probe's: `worker-start` panes inherit the worktree name as their title, so they are indistinguishable from the operator's own agent pane, and each of the seven hand-runs re-typed the keep-list from memory. So take a `snapshot --worktree <path> --out <f>` BEFORE the probe and the keep-list is recorded rather than remembered; a candidate is then a pane in that worktree, absent from the baseline, and not the caller's own — the caller's handle is re-read live from `orca terminal show` (which with no `--terminal` returns the caller's pane) and the run REFUSES rather than proceeding when it cannot identify itself. **It settles before it closes**: an unsettled dispatch wedges its terminal permanently (`worker-abandon`/`worker-stop` both answer `dispatch_not_found` — upstream stablyai/orca#13005), so each candidate is looked up in `orchestration worker-list` (used rather than `task-list`, which needs a bound Run) and an unsettled one gets `task-update --status completed` first; a settle that FAILS leaves the pane open and says so, because a wedged terminal you can still see beats one you cannot. Every close carries an explicit `--terminal` — the CLI takes it optionally and a bare `terminal close` closes the CALLER'S pane. **Absence from the baseline is subtraction, and subtraction alone cannot tell the probe's panes from the operator's** — so a candidate is closed only when a `worker-list` row positively identifies it. One with no worker row is