h-mad/SKILL.md:3207:- `h_mad_new_gate.py` — verdict-token gate scaffold: `scaffold()` + CLI printing `SCAFFOLD: WROTE name=<slug> token=<TOKEN> files=3` or `SCAFFOLD: REFUSED reason=<r>`, exit 0 on a write / 2 on a refusal. Refusal reasons: `bad_slug`, `bad_token`, `token_taken`, `would_overwrite`. Emits three files — `scripts/h_mad_<slug>.py`, `tests/test_h_mad_<slug>.py`, `tests/mutation-specs/<slug>.json` — and prints the registry line to paste here. **Counted 2026-08-25 from the scripts themselves: 20 distinct verdict tokens, 18 of them sharing one contract.** The code was never what cost time; three invariants were, and this emits them by construction with the tests that pin them: (1) a cannot-judge carries **no counts**, because a zero is byte-identical to "nothing was measured" and reads as clean — the shape that let a heading-less report score `must_count=0` and CLEAR the Phase-5 gate (#39); (2) **exit 0 on any verdict**, since a FAIL is a successful measurement and non-zero is reserved for operational failure, which is what lets a caller read the token rather than `$?`; (3) the docs table is **pinned bidirectionally** — every detail line the script can print has a remedy row here and vice versa. The generated suite is deliberately RED until the registry line is pasted, because the doc step is the one most easily skipped. The emitted mutation spec is ALL_CAUGHT out of the box; if it ever is not, the scaffold is shipping the appearance of coverage. Stdlib-only.
