evel `"target_command"` argv prefix), which changes the question from **did the suite go red** to **did THAT test bite** — the discrimination `ALL_CAUGHT` structurally cannot express, since a mutant that dies on a crash, a timeout or an unrelated assertion is otherwise byte-identical to the guard biting. A named test that PASSES while the suite goes red is a **survivor**, and the detail line names what actually bit; the named test is also required green before the mutation is applied, because a kill credited against an already-failing pin measures nothing and the whole-suite baseline cannot see one red pin. Caught and survived mutations both print a `mechanism:` detail line naming the killer, and a refused anchor prints `hint:` lines — near misses with line numbers when it matched 0 times, the match locations when it matched more than once — because the REFUSED verdict was always right and the recovery was a manual re-grep. A mutation whose `find` occurs verbatim inside its own `replace` is **diagnosed, never refused**: every substring of the original survives such a mutation, so an assertion shaped `assert "<text>" in …` cannot see it, and that is reported as a `self-matching:` detail line under `--check-anchors` and appended to the `mechanism:` line of any survivor — including the untargeted branch, which previously printed a bare name and nothing else. A backlog row asked for a refusal here; measured against the corpus on 2026-09-14 it would have refused **21 of 874** committed mutations, every one a legitimate insertion that is caught (`order = order + order` appended to a sort, `default=False,` added to an argparse line, a frontmatter `name:` renamed), so the diagnosis is kept and the refusal is refuted. It rides detail lines rather than the summary for the same reason `unclassifiable=` had to ride an existing verdict word: the `ANCHORS:` summary is asserted by exact string in the suite and scored by an ordered substring `case` in the pre-push hook whose default arm ALLOWS the push. Relative spec `root` values resolve against the spec file's directory, not the caller's cwd, so committed specs remain portable across checkouts and correct from git worktrees. A named-test kill is then classified further, because "that test failed" still covers two events wearing one `1 failed`: the guard's assertion bit, or the mutant CRASHED before the property was reached (the two measured cases are an unbound-variable crash tripping `assert r.returncode == 0`, and a 60-second `TimeoutExpired`). The scoring run's output is scanned STRUCTURALLY — `File "…"` frames attributed by basename to the mutated file, then a terminal exception line — never by a bare `Traceback` search, which fires on the echoed source of any test whose subject IS a crash. **Tier 1 (`SyntaxError`/`IndentationError`/`TabError` in the mutated file) is REFUSED**, on the same reasoning as the collection break: a file that did not parse cannot have exercised a guard, so it measured NOTHING — and unlike a collection break it is otherwise invisible, because a script consumed by `subprocess.run` can be syntax-broken with pytest's collection fully intact. **Tier 2 (every other exception) is ANNOTATED and COUNTED but never refused**: the `mechanism:` line carries `(crash: NameError in <file>)` and the summary carries `crash_kills=N`. Tier 2 is deliberately not a refusal because a mutation that strips a None-check makes the code raise `AttributeError` and the test asserting the graceful message then fails — there the crash IS the property violation, and the harness cannot tell that from a pre-property crash, so it prints the judgement rather than making it. `crash_kills=0` means "none found", not "none there": a test written `assert r.returncode == 0` with no message discards the child's stderr, and `TimeoutExpired` (no `Error`/`Exception` in its name, attributed to the test file) and the untargeted no-`test` branch are not classified at all. **`crash_kills=` is APPENDED to the end of the summary line** — a subset of `caught`, not a new outcome — so every consumer that parses that line by substring from the left is unaffected; the rest of what is new on a measured verdict is still an additional detail line. It also purges cached bytecode around every run: CPython invalidates a `.pyc` on (source mtime, size), a mutation is frequently byte-size-IDENTICAL, and it lands inside the same filesystem-mtime second as the previous run — so both invalidation inputs match, the stale bytecode is reused, the mutant never executes and the run reports `survived` while the file on disk is genuinely mutated (measured 4 false survivors in 6 trials before the fix). Reporting only: whether the mechanism that fired is the mechanism the spec claims stays with the author. Stdlib-only. With `--isolate`, single-spec runs, `--sweep` and `--check-anchors` run in a temporary detached worktree with dirty files copied in, spec paths rebased, and cleanup on exit or SIGINT/SIGTERM, preserving the original working tree. A row may declare `"requires": [argv...]`, probed in the spec root with a 30-second bound before applying it; a nonzero exit, timeout or missing executable records `host_skipped`, excluded from scoring, and an all-skipped run is `UNREADABLE`. Opt-in `--reachability` traces each named Python test on the original tree and appends `unreachable_kills=` for caught mutations whose original span never executed; unmeasured rows are reported separately and verdicts stay unchanged.
h-mad/SKILL.md:3258:- `h_mad_archreview_cycle.py` — Pass `--tree-snapshot <prompt>.tree.json` to `score` and treat `ARCHREVIEW-TREE: CHANGED` as a finding: “the review mutated what it measured”; this advisory never changes the recorded verdict or exit code. ONE Phase-6a-prime cycle, staged and scored: `stage` substitutes the reviewer template and prints the exact `exec agy` command, `score` runs the gates in the order that is the contract — **evidence FIRST, then verdict, then record, then READ BACK**. Prints `ARCHREVIEW: STAGED prompt=… base=… head=… bytes=N` or `ARCHREVIEW: READY_TO_MERGE|WITH_FIXES|NO tools=N recorded=yes` (exit 0), and `OVERSIZE`/`NO_EVIDENCE`/`LOW_EVIDENCE_CLEAN`/`NO_VERDICT`/`NOT_RECORDED`/`UNSUBSTITUTED`/`DEGENERATE_RANGE`/`UNREADABLE` (exit 2). **`OVERSIZE` refuses a prompt no delivery surface accepts and writes NO file** — `stage()` measured nothing while `h_mad_assemble_audit.py` held the measured ceiling one file over, so a ~690 KB prompt was staged, reported `STAGED`, and was then refused by the surface, which reads as "6a-prime failed" rather than "the prompt was never delivered"; the gate runs AFTER the output-contract prepend because that is the body a surface receives, and `--vh-tail N` is the remedy it names. **`LOW_EVIDENCE_CLEAN` is `NO_EVIDENCE` at the floor this channel's contract sets rather than at zero**: the reviewer template says "you must also WRITE your report file with `run_command`. That is the only write you may make" and asks for no `.done` marker, so `DELIVERY_FLOOR = 1` and a reviewer that read nothing but obeyed the delivery instruction used to score `tools=1` and pass. Derived from the CONTRACT, never from tool names — `h_mad_review_evidence.scan` knows none on purpose, and classifying calls as reads-vs-writes there would re-create the false zero that hardcoding `view_file|grep_search` produced. Asymmetric on purpose: only READY_TO_MERGE is held to the floor, because findings are evidence of reading and a clean is