h-mad/SKILL.md:3259:- `h_mad_baseline_sha.py` — the Phase-5c baseline sha, derived and self-checked: `derive()` + CLI printing `BASELINE: OK sha=<40-hex> branch=<b> trunk=<t>`, `BASELINE: UNVERIFIED reason=no_impl_plan candidate=<sha>`, or `BASELINE: NONE reason=no_commits_on_branch` — exit 0 on a verdict — and `BASELINE: UNREADABLE reason=<r>` (exit 2) when nothing could be derived. **Only `OK` carries `sha=`**; `UNVERIFIED` reports the unvouched value as `candidate=` instead, so a caller scraping `sha=` cannot receive a value nothing stands behind — the defect this script exists for was a wrong sha that looked exactly like a right one. 6a-prime and 5f both consume a 5c sha and nothing produced it, so it was produced by hand as `git merge-base main <branch>`, which returns 5c's PARENT (J41). Derived rather than stored in state for two reasons that both cut the same way: a stored sha does not survive a rebase, and a stored sha cannot tell you it is wrong — whereas "the branch's first commit is 5c" is a protocol invariant with an observable consequence (that commit touches an impl-plan), so a violated assumption surfaces as `UNVERIFIED` instead of a confident wrong answer. Stdlib-only.
