h-mad/SKILL.md:3262:- `h_mad_failure_diff.py` — **did the change cause this failure, or does the base fail the same way?** (row 2364). `run --base <ref> [--subject DIR] [--repeat N] [--python EXE] [--timeout S] [--control-parent DIR] [--log-dir DIR] -- <pytest selection…>` runs one selection, relative to the subject's toplevel, on the subject tree and on a throwaway detached worktree at the base. Runs alternate subject then control on each repeat so load lands on both sides, and they never overlap. The control takes its own suite lock, but the h-mad conftest sweeps shared `/tmp/audit_*_run*` files at session end, so two sessions at once race on any toplevels; other sessions' suites on the machine race the same way, which is one reason `--repeat` and FLAKY exist. Each run's output is kept and named on its `RUN:` line (`log=`), and pytest runs in its own process group, killed whole on a timeout or a signal. The control is removed on return, exception and SIGTERM/SIGHUP/SIGINT, and only its own worktree entry is deleted, never by a repo-wide prune. `logs --run <log> --control <log | id list>` compares two stored readings; an id list is one node id per line, like `baseline_failures.txt`. Node ids are read from pytest's short test summary only, never from captured output. It prints one `SUBJECT_ONLY <id>`, `BOTH <id>`, `CONTROL_ONLY <id>` or `FLAKY <id> subject=k/N control=k/N` line each, then `FAILDIFF: SAME|NEW|FLAKY|FIXED_ONLY subject_only=… both=… control_only=… flaky=… runs=N` (exit 0, 1, 3, 0) or `FAILDIFF: UNREADABLE reason=<r> side=<s>` (exit 2). A test that fails in some runs of a side but not all, or whose failure kind varies across one side's runs, is FLAKY and never attributed; NEW outranks FLAKY. FAILED and ERROR ids are both kept, and a test that FAILED on the base but ERRORs on the subject is SUBJECT_ONLY. Fail closed: a crash, a timeout, a collection error, a `-x`/`--maxfail` run that stopped early, an all-skipped selection, a `SUITE: BUSY` refusal, a mis